            <h1>Corporate Data Warehouse:<br>Medallion Architecture & EDA</h1>
            <p>Design and construction of an end-to-end data pipeline. From data governance and naming conventions implementation, to raw file ingestion (CRM/ERP), intensive T-SQL cleansing, dimensional modeling, and Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA).</p>
            
            <div class="tech-stack">
                <span class="tech-tag"><i class="fas fa-server"></i> SQL Server</span>
                <span class="tech-tag"><i class="fas fa-code"></i> T-SQL (Stored Procedures)</span>
                <span class="tech-tag"><i class="fas fa-book"></i> Data Governance</span>
                <span class="tech-tag"><i class="fas fa-layer-group"></i> Medallion Architecture</span>
                <span class="tech-tag"><i class="fas fa-project-diagram"></i> Star Schema</span>
                <span class="tech-tag"><i class="fas fa-chart-line"></i> EDA</span>
            </div>
        </header>

        <div class="doc-container">

            <!-- SECTION 0: DATA GOVERNANCE -->
            <section class="doc-section" data-aos="fade-up">
                <div class="doc-title">
                    <i class="fas fa-book"></i>
                    <h2>Data Governance & Naming Conventions</h2>
                </div>
                <div class="doc-text">
                    <p>A robust Data Warehouse requires a rigorous standard. Strict <em>Naming Conventions</em> rules using <code>snake_case</code> were implemented to ensure consistency across all logical schemas and facilitate data lineage traceability between source systems (CRM/ERP) and analytical tables.</p>
                </div>

                <ul class="qa-list">
                    <li class="qa-item">
                        <i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: var(--primary);"></i>
                        <div><strong>Raw Tables Standardization (Bronze & Silver)</strong>Entities maintain their source system name to ensure traceability. Pattern: <code>&lt;sourcesystem&gt;_&lt;entity&gt;</code> (e.g., <code>crm_customer_info</code>).</div>
                    </li>
                    <li class="qa-item">
                        <i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: var(--primary);"></i>
                        <div><strong>Business Modeling (Gold)</strong>Final tables use descriptive names aligned with the business domain, utilizing the prefixes <code>dim_</code> for Dimensions and <code>fact_</code> for Facts.</div>
                    </li>
                    <li class="qa-item">
                        <i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: var(--primary);"></i>
                        <div><strong>Technical Metadata & Auditing</strong>Every system-generated column for auditing purposes starts with the prefix <code>dwh_</code> (e.g., <code>dwh_create_date</code>). Every surrogate key uses the suffix <code>_key</code>.</div>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <!-- SECTION 1: ARCHITECTURE -->
            <section class="doc-section" data-aos="fade-up">
                <div class="doc-title">
                    <i class="fas fa-sitemap"></i>
                    <h2>Architecture Design & ETL Methodology</h2>
                </div>
                <div class="doc-text">
                    <p>Integrating concepts from <em>Kimball</em> and <em>Data Lakehouses</em>, a logical <strong>Medallion Architecture</strong> was designed in SQL Server. This approach guarantees the Separation of Concerns (SoC) between raw ingestion, transformation, and data consumption.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Data Layer</th>
                                <th>Objective & Object Type</th>
                                <th>Transformations Applied</th>
                                <th>Load Method</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong><i class="fas fa-box" style="color: #cd7f32;"></i> Bronze Layer</strong></td>
                                <td>Traceability. Physical Tables (Raw Tables). Data stored "As-Is".</td>
                                <td>None (As-Is).</td>
                                <td>Full Load (Truncate & Insert).</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong><i class="fas fa-filter" style="color: #c0c0c0;"></i> Silver Layer</strong></td>
                                <td>Cleansing and preparation. Physical Tables (Cleaned Tables).</td>
                                <td>Data Cleansing, Normalization, Standardization, and Derived Columns.</td>
                                <td>Full Load (Truncate & Insert).</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong><i class="fas fa-star" style="color: #ffbd2e;"></i> Gold Layer</strong></td>
                                <td>Business consumption (BI & Analytics). Analytical Views.</td>
                                <td>Data Integration, Aggregations, Business Logic, and Data Modeling (Star Schema).</td>
                                <td>None (Real-time Views).</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                
